**Solution:**
When two balls collide, they reverse directions but retain their speed. Since the balls are identical, this is physically equivalent to the balls passing through each other without changing velocity. Thus, each of the 5 right-moving balls will "interact" (collide) with each of the 5 left-moving balls exactly once. The total number of collisions is calculated as:

\[
5 \text{ (right-moving)} \times 5 \text{ (left-moving)} = 25 \text{ collisions.}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{25}

**Solution:**
```python
n, a, b = map(int, input().split())
c = 0
denied = 0

for group in map(int, input().split()):
    if group == 1:
        if a > 0:
            a -= 1
        elif b > 0:
            b -= 1
            c += 1
        elif c > 0:
            c -= 1
        else:
            denied += 1
    else:
        if b > 0:
            b -= 1
        else:
            denied += 2

print(denied)
```
This code tracks available tables (a, b) and two-seaters with one person (c). For each group, it follows the seating priority rules and updates the state accordingly. Denied people are counted based on the inability to seat the group. The algorithm efficiently processes each group in O(n) time, suitable for the input constraints.

